course: dictionary
lesson: 119
title: Adore: love, like and worship
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: You do not need to know every word to understand a text. Look at the words around it, because they are its context. «She adores her baby brother.» She is gentle with him and wants to be near him, so adores must mean loves him very much.
explain: Adore is a strong, warm word. It is stronger than like. «I adore chocolate cake» means you like it very much, much more than just liking it. Use it when your feelings are very big.
explain: In church, adore means to worship and praise. The carol «O come, let us adore him» means let us worship him. Christians speak of the shepherds and wise men coming to adore the baby Jesus.
explain: «Adoring fans» are fans who love someone very much, and «adorable» means very cute and lovable, as in «an adorable puppy». Adore changes its form, so check the ending to see how it is being used.
fact: Adore is a verb: adore, adores, adored, adoring.
fact: «Adore» means to love someone very much, or to like something very much.
fact: «Adorable» means very cute and lovable, like a puppy or a baby.
fact: In church, «adore» means to worship and praise God.
fact: Spelling: before -able the e usually drops, so adore becomes adorable.
exercise: «She adores her baby brother.» What does adores mean here? | Ignores on purpose | Is bored of | Hurts gently | *Loves very much
exercise: Which word means very cute and lovable in «an adorable puppy»? | *adorable | noticeable | puppy | wise
exercise: «I adore chocolate cake.» Does adore mean the same as like here? | *Yes, but much more strongly, like very much | No, it means to hate | No, it means to bake | No, it means to worship
test: In «O come, let us adore him», what does adore mean? | *Worship and praise | Cook for | Hide from | Laugh at
test: Which word is the adjective meaning very cute and lovable? | adores | *adorable | adored | adore
exam: Which word fits the gap in «The ___ fans waited all night for the singer.»? | adore | adorable | adores | *adoring
